    "post_rendered": "Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" > be a triangle and its bisector at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\"  width=\"13\" height=\"13\" > cuts its circumcircle at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/1/c61f1e8be9d73f590167b39417e42bbaa1787fe0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D.$\"  width=\"19\" height=\"12\" > Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > be the incenter of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/f/95fdfbb0362c49460ab4b7ef61acc59d3b0fac79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"45\" height=\"16\" > <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\"  width=\"19\" height=\"12\" > be the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/1/ce1f5a2ad34bb6fb5169f99e437b9f3e21d1887e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"32\" height=\"16\" > <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > is the symmetric to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" > with respect to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\"  width=\"19\" height=\"12\" > (Assuming <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > is in the circumcircle). Extend <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/3/783d500281f8f46167dff3b071c543d42150e67f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DP$\"  width=\"29\" height=\"12\" > until it cuts the circumcircle again at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/4/0b4a6d41204181432504f1108ded79e4a49e0e03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N.$\"  width=\"19\" height=\"12\" > Prove that among segments <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/c/88cd298c1af9177518695f8419a66e4dbe337127.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AN, BN, CN$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"106\" height=\"16\" >,</span> there is a segment that is the sum of the other two.",
    "post_canonical": "Let $ABC$ be a triangle and its bisector at $A$ cuts its circumcircle at $D.$ Let $I$ be the incenter of triangle $ABC,$ $M$ be the midpoint of $BC,$ $P$ is the symmetric to $I$ with respect to $M$ (Assuming $P$ is in the circumcircle). Extend $DP$ until it cuts the circumcircle again at $N.$ Prove that among segments $AN, BN, CN$, there is a segment that is the sum of the other two.",
